DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggeettrreessuuiidd() returns the real UID, the effective UID, and the saved set‐
       user‐ID  of the calling process, in the arguments _r_u_i_d, _e_u_i_d, and _s_u_i_d,
       respectively.   ggeettrreessggiidd()  performs  the  analogous  task   for   the
       process’s group IDs.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  success,  zero is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is
       set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       EEFFAAUULLTT One of the arguments specified an address  outside  the  calling
              program’s address space.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       None.  These calls also appear on HP‐UX and some of the BSDs.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Linux 2.1.44, glibc 2.3.2.

       The  original  Linux ggeettrreessuuiidd() and ggeettrreessggiidd() system calls supported
       only 16‐bit user and group IDs.  Subsequently, Linux 2.4  added  ggeettrree‐‐
       ssuuiidd3322()  and  ggeettrreessggiidd3322(),  supporting 32‐bit IDs.  The glibc ggeettrree‐‐
       ssuuiidd() and ggeettrreessggiidd() wrapper functions transparently  deal  with  the
       variations across kernel versions.

